<!-- agent-rules/rules/011-quality-gate.md -->

# 011 — Quality gate and evidence

## Gate

- **011.1** The gate is one command from the repository root: `agent-rules/scripts/gate.sh`.
  Steps: agent setup (stack installed, managed files unchanged, rule index and `.cursorrules` up
  to date), specification checks (registry, labels, coverage, questions, bugs, links), spec index
  freshness, project review page freshness, "code changed without specifications", lint of the
  OpenAPI contracts in `CONTRACTS`, the build and test steps (`BUILD_STEPS` or `BUILD_CMD` in
  `agent-rules/project.sh`).
  It does not stop at the first failure and prints a summary with the tail of every failed step.
- **011.2** Exit codes: `0` — every step passed; `1` — at least one step failed; `2` — no
  failures but at least one step was skipped or not run. A step marked `N/A` does not apply to the
  project by a setting in `agent-rules/project.sh` (for example `CONTRACTS="none"`); it is not a
  skip, and only the developer decides that a step does not apply.
- **011.3** **A skipped step is not a passed step.** "N passed, M skipped" is an incomplete
  gate. Report it as "Gate: not complete — <step> not run" with the exact reason and command
  output. Forbidden wording: "gate green, functional skipped", "all passed except skipped".
- **011.4** Before saying "done", run the gate and check the business logic, not only
  compilation. A failed step means the task is not done.
- **011.5** If a step needs an environment (SDK, build tool, Docker, private package registries,
  dependencies), bring it up with the documented command (`111-testing.md`) and run the step. Before
  starting or restarting a shared database or stack, check that no other session uses it; never
  touch a database instance you did not start.
- **011.6** If the environment cannot be brought up (missing access, secret or tool), stop and
  report which step did not run, why (with the command output) and what is needed.
- **011.7** A green gate means "ready for code review", not "ready for the developer".

## Tests

- **011.8** The test kinds, frameworks and tools of this stack are in `111-testing.md`; the project's
  own list of test kinds is in `specifications/features-test/README.md`.
- **011.9** Clients of external systems are tested against a stub or mock, never against a
  partner's production; contract assumptions in the stub are labelled "not verified" until a real
  response confirms them (`009-integrations.md`).
- **011.10** Every task has a test document `specifications/features-test/<TASK-ID>-TEST.md`
  that maps acceptance criteria to tests or recorded checks and lists what is not covered;
  `TEST_COVERAGE.yaml` keeps the per-task status (`planned`, `in_progress`, `done`, `n/a`). Coverage
  is tracked separately from progress.

## Checks beyond tests

- **011.11** Verify by running when you can: start the application locally and exercise the real
  flow (call the endpoint, open the page), and put the actual result (HTTP status and body, what the
  page showed, console errors) into the report. A unit test supplements this check, it
  does not replace it.
- **011.12** Before blaming your change for a failure, compare with the baseline: run the same
  check without your change; re-run a suspected flaky test separately and several times.
- **011.13** "Builds" and "runs" are different results; report them separately.
- **011.14** Remove test data you created (database rows, partner test cases, browser state) after a check.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/012-code-review.md -->

# 012 — Code review

- **012.1** Every piece of work goes through at least one code review before the developer checks
  it: a review session (`/code-review` in Claude Code) or the developer's own review comments.
- **012.2** The review runs in a **separate session** with a fresh context, never in the
  session that wrote the code. The writing session does not start `/code-review` itself.
- **012.3** When the work is finished, the writing session sets the tasks to `code_review`,
  gives the developer a summary and a ready-to-paste prompt for the review session, built from
  `agent-rules/prompts/code-review.md`, as a code block in the chat (not as a file).
- **012.4** The prompt lists: task IDs in `code_review`; changed and new files (from
  `git status` and `git diff HEAD`); specs and test documents to check against the acceptance
  criteria; what to look at closely (risks, decisions taken along the way, places without tests);
  the status order `review_fixes` → fixes → gate → `ready_for_review`; the prohibitions (no commits,
  pushes or tracker actions without the developer's word).
- **012.5** The review session runs `/code-review` on the changes, sets `review_fixes`, fixes
  the findings, re-runs the gate, records the review in the work log and changelog, and sets
  `ready_for_review` (or `code_review` again if the developer asks for another round).
- **012.6** The developer's review comments in a message count as a review: fix them, re-run the
  gate, then hand the work over.
- **012.7** Review only the files of the tasks under review; changes of another session in the
  working tree are reported, not modified.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/013-git-and-sync.md -->

# 013 — Git and repository sync

## Sync before any work

- **013.1** At the start of every request: detect the branch, check the working tree, run
  `git fetch --prune`, find the upstream and compare. If the branch is behind and the tree is clean,
  `git pull --ff-only` and say that the code was updated.
- **013.2** Also list remote branches (`git branch -r`): active work may live on a feature
  branch, not on the main branch.
- **013.3** If the sync cannot complete cleanly (no upstream, dirty tree blocking the update,
  diverged history, conflicts, network or authentication error) — stop all work and ask the developer.
  Never stash, commit, reset, merge or rebase on your own.
- **013.4** Also compare with the branches listed in `001-project-context.md` (001.9); merge new
  commits from them only with the developer's approval.

## Branches and commits

- **013.5** Work on the branch agreed with the developer; for a tracker task that is the task
  branch (013.12). Never switch, create or merge branches silently.
- **013.6** Commit and push only on the developer's explicit word, after showing what will be
  committed and pushed.
- **013.7** Commit subject in conventional style, shorter than 70 characters, with the registry
  task ID and the tracker ID when there is one, for example
  `fix(ORDER-01-BE, TRK-123): register OrderController`.
- **013.8** The commit author comes from the machine's git config. Never pass `--author`,
  `-c user.*` or `GIT_AUTHOR_*`. No AI co-author trailers (`Co-Authored-By`) and no
  "Generated with" lines.
- **013.9** Stage files explicitly; never stage `.env*`, `*.pem`, `*.key`, `*-sa.json`,
  `credentials*`, `.secrets`, `.DS_Store`, IDE folders or local tool output.
- **013.10** Forbidden: `--force` and `--force-with-lease`, `--no-verify`, amending a pushed
  commit, deleting branches, `git reset --hard`.
- **013.11** Stop on any git error, print it verbatim and name the repository and branch.

## One branch per tracker task

- **013.12** When you work on a tracker task, every repository you change for it uses the same
  branch, named after the task: `<tracker label>-<task id>_<task title>_<developer name>`. The
  task title is the title as written in the tracker with every run of characters other than letters
  and digits replaced by one `-` and no `-` at either end; the developer name is the developer's
  full name with spaces replaced by `-`. Example: ClickUp task `869fkhfxq` "Title: Add API
  Pre-check for Partner Customer Eligibility", developer Anton Bashmakov →
  `CU-869fkhfxq_Title-Add-API-Pre-check-for-Partner-Customer-Eligibility_Anton-Bashmakov`.
- **013.13** Before changing a repository for the task, look for the task branch there
  (`git branch -r`). If it exists, continue on it; if not, create it from the up-to-date main
  branch and tell the developer. All work for the task (implementation, review fixes, follow-ups,
  specification updates) goes to that branch in every repository, never to a branch with another
  name.
- **013.14** If the task branch cannot be used (uncommitted work on another branch, the session or
  tool is pinned to a different branch, the title is unclear), stop and ask the developer instead of
  picking another name.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/014-parallel-sessions.md -->

# 014 — Parallel agent sessions

- **014.1** Several agent sessions may work in the same working tree. Before editing a shared
  file (registry, questions, changelog, bugs), re-read it; change only your own entries; never roll
  back changes made by another session.
- **014.2** Before creating a "new" file, check that it does not exist yet (`ls`): writing over
  a file created by another session loses its work.
- **014.3** IDs that parallel branches create (migration IDs and similar) follow the stack rules so
  that parallel sessions never collide. Do not take question or bug numbers another session has already
  used: re-read the file right before adding an entry.
- **014.4** Stop your own processes by PID only, never with `pkill -f <pattern>`.
- **014.5** Do not restart or migrate a database, dev server or stack started by another session;
  use your own (separate test database, separate port) or ask.
- **014.6** If the gate fails because of another session's changes, say so explicitly and do
  not change that code.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/015-destructive-actions.md -->

# 015 — Actions that need the developer's explicit confirmation

The project's own list of protected setup, deployment and production files is in the project-owned
rule `115-protected-files.md`.

- **015.1** Deleting directories or files; `git reset --hard`; force push; commit and push.
- **015.2** Deleting, renaming or editing applied database migrations; clearing a database;
  restoring a database from a dump.
- **015.3** Altering, creating or removing CI configuration (`.github/` and the CI files listed in
  `115-protected-files.md`).
- **015.4** Creating or changing project setup and deployment files listed in
  `115-protected-files.md`. Add dependencies only within a task (`004-dependencies-and-configuration.md`).
- **015.5** Changing production configuration (files, cloud resources, IAM, schedulers, queues)
  listed in `115-protected-files.md`.
- **015.6** Calling production systems of external partners; writing to the task tracker.
- **015.7** Deleting or rewriting `specifications/` content: source task texts, registry, work logs,
  bug history, ADRs. Rewriting the PRD without keeping history.
- **015.8** Changing anything outside this repository: the developer's machine and tool
  configuration (package manager settings with credentials, shell profiles, `~/.claude/`, IDE
  settings), other repositories on the machine, system files. If a task seems to need such a change
  (for example to make the build work), describe it and let the developer make or confirm it.
- **015.9** Editing a file managed by the boilerplate (listed in `agent-rules/boilerplate.manifest`)
  or running the installer with `--force`; propose the change for the boilerplate repository
  instead (`019-rule-layers-and-updates.md`).

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/016-specifications-upkeep.md -->

# 016 — Keeping specifications current

The knowledge base is the memory of the project. An agent forgets everything between sessions; the
developer, the team and other sessions cannot see an agent's memory. Everything that matters
goes into `specifications/` in the same task as the code.

## What to update when

| Change | Update in the same task |
|---|---|
| Any work (feature, fix, review, refactoring) | `logs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md`, registry `status` and `progress_percent`, `product/CHANGELOG.md` |
| Behaviour of an endpoint or a flow | the task spec, the API contract, test document, `TEST_COVERAGE.yaml` |
| New or changed requirement from the tracker | `product/source-tasks/<id>.md` (new text verbatim, old kept), registry criteria with `[<TRK>-<id>]`, spec |
| Question to the client or a decision of the developer | `questions/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md`; when answered — decisions table and the affected criteria with `[Q-NN]` |
| Architecture or design decision | a new ADR in `architecture/adr/`, `architecture/SYSTEM_OVERVIEW.md` |
| Database schema | `architecture/SYSTEM_OVERVIEW.md` (data model section), migration |
| Partner or tracker behaviour observed in a real response | `integrations/<SYSTEM>.md`, "Real API findings" |
| Personal data stored or sent | `security/PERSONAL_DATA.md` |
| Defect found | `bugs/BUGS.md` and a criterion `[BUG-NNN]` in the affected task |
| New or renamed spec or test document | `python3 agent-rules/scripts/gen_spec_index.py` |
| Registry, `product/source-tasks/README.md` or `questions/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md` | `python3 agent-rules/scripts/gen_project_review.py` (skill `project-review`); with required translations first the Russian texts in `specifications/i18n/PROJECT_REVIEW.ru.yaml` (`--missing`) |
| `AGENTS.md` or a project rule in `agent-rules/rules/` | `python3 agent-rules/scripts/gen_rule_index.py`, then `python3 agent-rules/scripts/gen_cursorrules.py` |
| A managed file (core or stack rule, script, hook, shared skill) needs a change | nothing here: propose the change in the boilerplate repository (`019-rule-layers-and-updates.md`) |

## Rules

- **016.1** Update `specifications/` in the same task as the code; "later" does not happen.
- **016.2** Say "added a task" or "recorded the decision" only after it is in the files.
- **016.3** An agent's own memory is not a substitute for `specifications/`: write to the files
  first, memory last and only as a supplement.
- **016.4** Question numbers: next after the maximum in the Open section and the decisions
  table; bug numbers: next after the maximum in `BUGS.md`. Re-read the file right before writing.
- **016.5** Every work log starts with the plan (sources, decisions, how it will be checked)
  written before coding, then "Done", "Checks" (gate per step), "Code review" (date, separate
  session), "Statuses", "Findings and decisions".
- **016.6** Developer decisions are recorded with a date and a source.
- **016.7** An ADR is never rewritten: a changed decision gets a new ADR that supersedes the
  old one, and the old ADR is marked as superseded.

## Enforcement

- **016.8** The gate fails when code (`CODE_PATHS` in `agent-rules/project.sh`, minus
  `SPEC_EXEMPT_PATHS`) changed in the working tree but nothing under `specifications/` did
  (`agent-rules/scripts/spec_freshness.sh`).
- **016.9** The Claude Code Stop hook (`.claude/settings.json`) runs the same check and asks the
  agent once to update the specifications before finishing; if no update is needed, the agent
  states why in the work log.
- **016.10** The gate validates the registry, labels, coverage, questions, bugs, links and the
  spec index (`agent-rules/scripts/check_specs.py`, `gen_spec_index.py --check`).
- **016.11** `.cursorrules` is generated from `AGENTS.md` and `agent-rules/rules/` for Cursor and
  any pipeline that reads one rules file, and the rule index in `AGENTS.md` is generated from the
  rule files. Never edit either by hand: after changing the rules run
  `python3 agent-rules/scripts/gen_rule_index.py` and
  `python3 agent-rules/scripts/gen_cursorrules.py`; the gate fails when either is stale.
- **016.12** `specifications/PROJECT_REVIEW.html` shows the team and stakeholders the state of the
  project: tasks with status and readiness, open questions and decisions. It is generated by
  `agent-rules/scripts/gen_project_review.py` (Claude Code skill `.claude/skills/project-review/`);
  never edit it by hand. The page switches between English and Russian; Russian texts of the data
  live in `specifications/i18n/PROJECT_REVIEW.ru.yaml`. With `project.review_translation: required`
  in the registry the gate fails when a translation is missing or unused; otherwise missing texts are
  shown in English. The gate always fails when the page is stale.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/017-communication.md -->

# 017 — Communication and reports

- **017.1** All project files — rules, specifications, code comments, commit messages, work
  logs — are written in English. The chat language with a person follows that person's own
  instructions.
- **017.2** Be direct, practical and precise; do not hide uncertainty. Express status through
  facts ("Gate: failed", "Status: blocked", "Reason: …", "Next action: …"), without rhetorical
  sincerity markers ("honestly", "frankly", "to be honest").
- **017.3** Every ID mentioned to a person (task, bug, question, ADR, tracker task) is written as
  "[ID] — title", with the title taken from the project source. This applies to every mention.
- **017.4** For non-trivial technical choices state the chosen approach, the main alternative
  and why the chosen one is safer.
- **017.5** Final report of every task: repository sync (fetched, pulled, code changed,
  blocked and why); what was changed or found; files changed or inspected; verification performed
  (gate per step); remaining risks and follow-ups.
- **017.6** Use clear headings, short bullets and concrete file and function references; do
  not use the middle dot character (U+00B7) as a separator.
- **017.7** If access is missing (repository, registry, partner environment, task tracker, a file),
  say so immediately: what is missing, why it is needed and how to grant it.
- **017.8** Do not ask again about decisions the developer has already made; they are recorded in
  `questions/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md`.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/018-debugging-and-evidence.md -->

# 018 — Debugging and evidence

- **018.1** For any bug, failing test, unexpected behaviour or suspicious log, read the project
  code before drawing a conclusion. Logs, stack traces, screenshots and failing tests are entry
  points only.
- **018.2** Investigation order: sync → read the error → find the related source files →
  inspect the implementation → trace data and control flow → check tests, configuration, schema,
  migrations, generated code → only then explain the cause and propose or implement a fix.
- **018.3** Every important conclusion is tied to evidence: file and line, function, condition,
  configuration key, changeSet, test or commit. If evidence is incomplete, say exactly what is
  missing.
- **018.4** Deployment configuration often lives outside the repository (CI variables,
  runtime environment of the hosting, secret managers). Do not conclude production behaviour from
  repository defaults; check the live environment or mark the conclusion as unverified.
- **018.5** Before stating that a tool, SDK or service is missing, ask the tool itself
  (for example `java -version`, `node -v`, `mvn -v`, `docker info`, `gcloud auth list`) and
  list the places and commands checked; write "not found in these places", not "absent".

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/019-rule-layers-and-updates.md -->

# 019 — Rule layers, project overrides and boilerplate updates

## Layers

- **019.1** The agent setup of this repository comes from `ai-ready-boilerplate` in three layers:
  core rules `0xx` (every project), stack rules `1xx` (the stack named in
  `agent-rules/boilerplate.lock`) and project rules (`001`, `104`, `115` and `2xx`).
- **019.2** Managed files — core and stack rules, scripts, hooks, shared skills, spec templates —
  are listed with their checksums in `agent-rules/boilerplate.manifest`. Never edit them here:
  the gate step "agent / managed files" fails on a local edit. A change that is useful beyond this
  project goes to the boilerplate repository.
- **019.3** Project-owned files are never overwritten by an update: `AGENTS.md` outside its managed
  block, `CLAUDE.md`, `agent-rules/project.sh`, rules `001`, `104`, `115` and `2xx`, project skills
  (any `.claude/skills/` folder not in the manifest) and everything in `specifications/` except the
  `_TEMPLATE*.md` files.

## Project rules and overrides

- **019.4** A project rule lives in `agent-rules/rules/2NN-<topic>.md`. It overrides a core or
  stack rule only when it names the rule it overrides, for example "**201.1** Overrides 111.4: …";
  everywhere else the stricter rule applies.
- **019.5** After adding or renaming a rule, regenerate the rule index in `AGENTS.md` and
  `.cursorrules`: `python3 agent-rules/scripts/gen_rule_index.py` and
  `python3 agent-rules/scripts/gen_cursorrules.py`.

## Stack

- **019.6** If `agent-rules/boilerplate.lock` has no stack, do not write code: suggest a stack
  from the repository, ask the developer to choose, and install it (skill `setup-stack`). Never
  choose a stack without the developer's answer, and never switch the stack of a repository that
  already has one without the developer's explicit word.

## Updates

- **019.7** The boilerplate is updated only with its installer:
  `python3 <boilerplate>/install.py --update .` (skill `setup-stack`, "Update"). The installer
  overwrites managed files that are unchanged, refuses to overwrite a locally edited one and lists
  it, adds new files and removes managed files the boilerplate no longer has. Show the developer the
  resulting diff; it is committed like any other change.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/104-tech-stack.md -->

# 104 — Technology stack and versions (nextjs)

## Fixed versions (from `package.json` and `yarn.lock` of each package, 2026-10-31)

| Component | Version |
|---|---|
| Node.js | 22 (`engines.node` of `functions/package.json`); no CI or Dockerfile |
| Package manager | yarn 1 (classic); lock file `yarn.lock` in the root, `functions/`, `crm/`, `twa/`, `bot/`. Stale `package-lock.json` files sit next to them (Q-02) |
| Next.js | `crm/` 16.0.7, `twa/` 16.3.4; App Router |
| React, React DOM | `crm/` 19.2.0, `twa/` 19.2.8 |
| TypeScript | 5.9.3 in every package |
| Styling | `crm/`: Chakra UI 3.29.0 (theme in `crm/src/theme/`) with Tailwind CSS 4.1.17 installed; `twa/`: Tailwind CSS 4.3.3; class helper none; CSS Modules: no |
| Server state and data fetching | TanStack React Query (`crm/` 5.90.12, `twa/` 5.102.8) |
| API client and type generation | openapi-fetch + openapi-typescript (`crm/` 0.9.8 + 6.7.6, `twa/` 0.15.2 + 7.13.0, `functions/` openapi-typescript 7.x, `bot/` 0.9.x + 6.x) |
| Internationalisation | next-intl (`crm/` 4.5.8, `twa/` 4.14.4) |
| Forms and validation | no form library; controlled components |
| Component workshop and mocks | `crm/`: Storybook 10.1.4 (`crm/.storybook/`, stories in `crm/src/stories/`); `twa/`: none |
| Tests | `functions/`: Jest 30.2.0 + ts-jest 29.4.5 + supertest 7.1.4; `crm/`, `twa/`, `bot/`: none |
| Layout | Firebase: `functions/` + Next.js apps `crm/` and `twa/` + Telegram bot `bot/` (rules 107, 201) |
| Backend runtime | Firebase Cloud Functions gen 1 API with firebase-functions 5.1.1, firebase-admin 12.7.0, Express 4.21.2; Node.js `engines.node` 22 |
| Backend tests | Jest + ts-jest + supertest against the Firestore emulator, project `test-project` |
| AI | `openai` 7.19.0 SDK pointed at DeepSeek (`https://api.deepseek.com`) |
| Firebase CLI | firebase-tools not pinned in the root `package.json` (rule 201.4); global 14.25.1 on the developer machine, `crm/` dev dependency `^13.0.0`; Java 21 for the emulators; emulator project `test-project` |

## Rules

- **104.1** One package manager: `yarn`. Install with `yarn install --frozen-lockfile` in each
  package; only `yarn.lock` is updated. The `package-lock.json` files are not touched (Q-02).
- **104.2** Node.js major version is 22 (`NODE_MAJOR` in `agent-rules/project.sh`).
- **104.3** New dependencies are pinned to a version range the lock file resolves (never `latest` or
  `*`). Dependencies already declared as `latest`: none.
- **104.4** Environment files: committed, without secrets: `functions/.env.test-project` (synthetic
  values for the test emulator), `twa/.env.development` (fake bot token for the dev sign-in and how to set up the browser
  Telegram login, read by `next dev` only) and `twa/.env.production` (public values of the production build).
  Not committed: `functions/.env` (T-Bank terminal credentials, DeepSeek API key) and
  `functions/.env.local` (a real bot token for testing the browser Telegram login) and
  `twa/.env.development.local` (emulator URL or `DEV_API_PROXY` / `DEV_TUNNEL_HOST` for `next dev`; a `.env.local` would also be read by
  production builds) (rule 110.2).
- **104.5** API types: generated by `yarn openapi` in each package from its contract
  (`functions/resources/openapi/*.yml` → `functions/src/openapi/`, `crm/openapi/api.yml` →
  `crm/src/api/openapi/crm.d.ts`, `twa/openapi/*.yml` → `twa/src/api/openapi/`,
  `bot/resources/openapi/api.yml` → `bot/src/openapi/crm.d.ts`); the output is committed (rule 108).
- **104.6** Backend (rule 106): source in `functions/src`, contract
  `functions/resources/openapi/api.yml` and `models.yml`; no root `dev` script yet (rule 201.5);
  the functions emulator starts with `cd functions && yarn serve`; integration tests run with
  `firebase emulators:exec --project test-project --only firestore,functions 'yarn test'` from
  `functions/`.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/105-architecture.md -->

# 105 — Architecture and code style (nextjs)

## Structure

- **105.1** Follow the router the project uses (`app/` or `pages/`, rule 104); do not introduce the
  other one.
- **105.2** Route files (`page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, route handlers) are thin: they read params, pick
  the data and compose components. Business logic, API access and formatting live in their own
  modules.
- **105.3** Server and client components (App Router): add `"use client"` only to components that
  need state, effects or browser APIs, and keep it as far down the tree as possible.
- **105.4** All calls to backend APIs go through the project's API module (client plus hooks, rule
  108); components never call `fetch` on a backend URL directly.
- **105.5** Server data stays in the data-fetching library's cache (query keys, invalidation); it is
  not copied into global or component state.
- **105.6** Forms use the project's form library and the shared validators; client validation is a
  convenience, the backend still validates.

## Components and styling

- **105.7** Reuse the existing UI components before writing a new one; a new reusable component goes
  to the UI folder with a story covering its states when the project has Storybook (skill
  `add-component`).
- **105.8** Components are styled with Tailwind CSS utility classes in their markup (rule 104 names
  the version and the class helper, such as `clsx`). Use the theme variables or Tailwind theme tokens;
  do not add another styling system or hardcode theme values (colours, fonts, spacing) that exist as
  variables. A project without Tailwind keeps its existing approach until adding Tailwind is agreed
  with the developer as a task of its own; until then 105.16–105.18 do not apply to it. (The styling
  points 105.16–105.18 stand here, out of numeric order, so that 105.9–105.15 keep their numbers.)
- **105.16** No CSS classes for components: a component is styled only with Tailwind utilities in its
  markup. It gets no `*.module.css`, `*.css` or `*.scss` file and no named class in the global
  stylesheet. The global stylesheet holds only the Tailwind import, the theme (variables, theme
  tokens, keyframes), base styles of plain HTML elements and fonts.
- **105.17** Migrate on touch: when a task changes an existing component in any way (markup, text,
  styles, props or logic), the component's CSS Module moves to Tailwind in the same task — its classes
  are replaced with utilities, the module file and its import are removed, and the component looks the
  same at mobile and desktop width (its story when the project has Storybook, and a browser check,
  skill `verify-in-browser`). This includes modules that only set theme variables for descendants (a
  themed or branded area): the variables move to arbitrary properties in the markup
  (`[--color-text:var(--color-text-on-background-dark)]`). Components the task does not change keep
  their CSS Modules until a task changes them.
- **105.18** What looks hard to express with utilities is still written with Tailwind, not with a CSS
  class: keyframes become theme animations (Tailwind 4: `--animate-*` in `@theme` with its
  `@keyframes`; Tailwind 3: `theme.extend.keyframes` and `animation`), third-party or generated markup
  is styled with arbitrary variants on the element that wraps it (`[&_.slick-dot]:bg-(--color-primary)`).
  No `@utility`, `@layer components` or `@layer utilities` classes for components. If no Tailwind form
  exists, ask the developer before adding any other CSS.
- **105.9** Accessibility: semantic elements, a label for every input, `alt` text for meaningful
  images, everything reachable and usable with the keyboard.
- **105.10** Use `next/image`, `next/font` and `next/link` where the project uses them.

## Content, data and configuration

- **105.11** Copy and legal texts change exactly as the task says; legal documents (terms, PDFs)
  come from a named source recorded in the task. Do not rewrite surrounding text.
- **105.12** Money is computed from decimal values or minor units and formatted with the shared
  helpers (`Intl.NumberFormat`); dates are exchanged in UTC and formatted for display only (rules
  005.6, 005.7).
- **105.13** `process.env` is read in the documented places only; `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values are inlined
  into the browser bundle at build time (rule 110.1).
- **105.14** Redirects, rewrites and headers in `next.config.*` and the middleware are production
  routing and security: changing them needs confirmation (rule 115).
- **105.15** No `console.log` of API responses, form values or tokens in committed code (rule 110.3).

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/106-node-backend.md -->

# 106 — Node.js backend: controllers, services, imports (nextjs)

Applies when the repository has its own Node.js backend: Firebase Cloud Functions in `functions/`
(rule 107) or a server package on Express, Fastify or a similar framework. A Next.js app without its
own backend skips this rule; its route handlers stay thin (105.2, 108.6).

## Layers

- **106.1** The backend source (`functions/src/` with Firebase, `src/` of the server package
  otherwise) has these folders and nothing else at the top level apart from `index.ts`, `db.ts`,
  `auth.ts`, `env.ts` and `constants.ts`:

  | Folder | Holds |
  |---|---|
  | `controllers/<module>/` | Receives calls from clients and triggers, calls services, shapes the response |
  | `services/` | Data access (DAO) and external systems: the only code that talks to the database or a third party |
  | `services/events/` | Processors of outbox events (106.12) |
  | `models/` | Entity and DTO types; generated contract types are re-exported from here (106.11) |
  | `middleware/` | Express middleware shared by modules (locale, authorization) |
  | `scripts/` | Local tooling such as the emulator seed (rule 107.8) |
  | `test/` | `setup.ts`, `unit/`, `integration/` (rule 111) |

- **106.2** Dependencies point one way: `index.ts` → `controllers` → `services` → database or SDK.
  A service never imports a controller; a controller never touches the database, a collection or a
  query directly; `models/` and `constants.ts` import nothing from the other folders.

## Controller modules

- **106.3** A controller module is a folder of exactly three files:
  - `index.ts` re-exports the module's entry, for example `export {default as publicApi} from "./public";`
  - `imports.ts` imports every dependency of the module — framework, SDKs, services, constants,
    helpers — and re-exports them. It is the single place where a real implementation is replaced by
    its test double (106.8);
  - `<module>.ts` defines the Express app or the triggers and imports its dependencies only from
    `./imports` (types from `../../models` are the exception).

  ```ts
  // controllers/private/imports.ts
  import express from "express";
  import cors from "cors";
  import {firestore} from "../../db";
  import {isEmulated} from "../../env";
  import {api} from "../../services/utils";
  import {authorize} from "../../middleware/authorize";
  import ItemService from "../../services/ItemService";
  import RealPaymentService from "../../services/payments/PaymentService";
  import MockPaymentService from "../../services/payments/MockPaymentService";

  const PaymentService = isEmulated() ? MockPaymentService : RealPaymentService;

  export {express, cors, firestore, api, authorize, ItemService, PaymentService};
  ```

- **106.4** One module per audience or trigger kind, mounted under `/api/<module>`: `public` (no
  sign-in), `private` (signed-in user), `admin` (role-checked), `webhook` (third-party callbacks,
  signature verified first), `db` (Firestore, Pub/Sub and scheduled triggers). A new route goes to
  the module of its audience; a new module only for a new audience or trigger kind (skill
  `add-endpoint`).
- **106.5** A controller handler validates the input, checks authentication and roles, calls
  services, and answers with the shared `api` helper (`api.send`, `api.ok`, `api.badRequest`,
  `api.unauthorized`, `api.forbidden`, `api.notFound`, `api.error`, and `api.failure` in the catch
  block); error bodies are
  `{error: {code, message}}`. Every handler catches its errors, logs them with the functions logger
  and answers without internal details (005.5). Business rules that span several services live in a
  service, not in the handler.
- **106.6** Services are created once per module, at the top of `<module>.ts`, from the shared
  database handle: `const db = firestore(); const itemService = new ItemService(db);`. Controllers
  never call `admin.initializeApp()`, `db.settings()` or `new Firestore()` themselves (106.10).

## Services

- **106.7** One service per collection or table: `class <Entity>Service extends
  AbstractService<Entity>`, with its collection name from `COLLECTIONS` in `constants.ts`. The
  database handle comes through the constructor; the service never creates it. Queries,
  transactions, batched writes, increments and the mapping of stored documents to plain objects
  (string ids, timestamps to `Date`) live in the service and nowhere else.
- **106.8** A service for an external system (payments, messaging, e-mail) has a mock implementation
  `Mock<Name>Service` with the same public methods, next to it. The real one is replaced by the mock
  only in an `imports.ts` (106.3) or a service's own imports file (106.9), with the check
  `isEmulated()` from `env.ts` — never with `if` checks inside handlers or services, and never with
  `exports.X = …` reassignments.
- **106.9** A service that depends on other services or on external systems imports them through its
  own file `<Name>Service.import.ts`, by the same rules as a controller's `imports.ts`.

## Entry point, database handle and contracts

- **106.10** `db.ts` initializes the Admin SDK once and exports `firestore()`, which applies
  `DATABASE_ID` when the project uses a named database; `auth.ts` exports `auth()` and is imported
  only where the Auth SDK is needed (middleware, seed), because it loads ES modules that Jest on
  Node 22 cannot require. `env.ts` exports `isEmulated()`: true when
  the project id starts with `demo-` (the emulator project, rule 107.3), which a deployed function
  never has.
- **106.11** `index.ts` only wires: one HTTPS function per HTTP module, named after it, that strips
  the `/api/<module>` prefix and hands the request to the module's app; the triggers of `db`; and,
  under the emulator only, the function `api` that routes `/<module>/…` to each module for the local
  UI (rule 107.6). No logic in `index.ts`.
- **106.12** A state change that must cause a side effect (a message, a charge, another write)
  writes an outbox event in the same transaction; a `db` trigger hands it to its processor in
  `services/events/`. Processors are idempotent and record failures and retries on the event.
- **106.13** The HTTP API is described in an OpenAPI contract owned by the backend
  (`functions/resources/openapi/api.yml`, listed in `CONTRACTS`); types are generated from it into
  `src/openapi/` and re-exported from `models/`. The UI copies the contract and generates its own
  types (rule 108); a change of the API starts with the contract.

## Other Node.js frameworks

- **106.14** A backend on Express, Fastify or Koa outside Firebase follows the same layers, the same
  three files per module and the same `db.ts` and `env.ts`, with `isEmulated()` replaced by the
  project's test or local environment check. A framework with its own dependency injection (NestJS)
  keeps controllers and services as above but swaps test doubles through its container instead of
  `imports.ts`.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/107-firebase-emulators.md -->

# 107 — Firebase layout and local emulators (nextjs)

Applies when the project is deployed on Firebase (`firebase.json` at the repository root).

## Layout

- **107.1** The repository root holds only the Firebase configuration and the two packages:

  | Path | Holds |
  |---|---|
  | `firebase.json`, `.firebaserc` | Functions, hosting, emulators (107.4); project aliases |
  | `firestore.rules`, `firestore.indexes.json` | Security rules (107.10) and indexes |
  | `functions/` | Cloud Functions in TypeScript, laid out by rule 106; own `package.json` and lock file |
  | `ui/` | The Next.js app (rules 105, 108, 110); own `package.json` and lock file |
  | `package.json` | Scripts that start and test both packages and `firebase-tools` as a dev dependency; no runtime code |

- **107.2** `ui/` never imports from `functions/` and `functions/` never imports from `ui/`; shared
  shapes come from the backend contract (106.13, 108).
- **107.3** Run the Firebase CLI from the root `package.json` (`npx firebase …`) so every session uses
  the pinned `firebase-tools` version (rule 104), not a global install.

## Running locally on the emulators

- **107.4** The whole project runs on the Firebase Local Emulator Suite without any cloud resource.
  `firebase.json` has an `emulators` block with fixed ports for every Firebase product the project
  uses (at least `auth`, `functions` and `firestore`; `pubsub`, `storage`, `hosting` when used),
  `ui.enabled: true` and `singleProjectMode: true`.
- **107.5** Local runs and tests use the demo project id `demo-<project-slug>`
  (`EMULATOR_PROJECT` in `agent-rules/project.sh`): `npx firebase emulators:start --project
  demo-<slug>`. A demo project has no cloud counterpart, so nothing reaches a real project and a
  product without an emulator fails instead of touching production. With it, `isEmulated()` is true
  (106.10) and external systems use their mock services (106.8). Never run the emulators against
  the production project id.
- **107.6** One command starts everything: `<PKG> run dev` at the root builds the functions in
  watch mode, starts the emulators and starts `next dev` in `ui/`. The UI reaches the backend through
  the emulator-only function `api` (106.11): `ui/.env.development` (committed, no secrets) sets
  `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:<functions port>/demo-<slug>/<region>/api`. A UI
  that uses the Firebase client SDK connects it to the emulators (`connectAuthEmulator`,
  `connectFirestoreEmulator`) when `NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_EMULATORS=true`.
- **107.7** The functions emulator loads the compiled code from `functions/lib/`: build before
  starting it (the `dev` script runs `tsc --watch`), and after a change check that the emulator
  reloaded it before testing.
- **107.8** Local data is synthetic: `<PKG> run seed` writes it to the Firestore and Auth emulators
  with the Admin SDK (`functions/src/scripts/seed.ts`), and refuses to run when
  `FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST` is not set. Exported emulator data (`--export-on-exit`) stays out of git
  unless it is synthetic and the developer agreed to commit it.
- **107.9** Configuration of functions: values for the emulator are in `functions/.env.demo-<slug>`
  (committed, synthetic, no secrets — Firebase loads `.env.<projectId>`). Production values come
  from `functions/.env.<alias>` files that are not committed or from Secret Manager (`defineSecret`);
  `functions/.secret.local` and `.env.local` override them in the emulator and are never committed
  (rule 110.2).

## Security rules, deployment and housekeeping

- **107.10** When every read and write goes through the functions (rule 106), `firestore.rules`
  denies all client access. Opening a path to the client SDK is a change of its own: rules written
  for it and tested on the Firestore emulator, and confirmed by the developer (rule 115).
- **107.11** Hosting serves `ui/` (`"source": "ui"`, `frameworksBackend` with a fixed region); its
  rewrites send `/api/<module>/**` to the function of that module before the catch-all `**`. Changes
  to `firebase.json`, `.firebaserc`, rules and indexes need confirmation (rule 115).
- **107.12** The emulators need Node.js (the `engines.node` of `functions/package.json`) and the Java
  version the pinned `firebase-tools` requires. Start your own emulators, note the PID and stop them by
  it (014.4); emulator ports that are already taken belong to another session (014.5): ask, or run
  your emulators with other ports in a copy of `firebase.json` passed with `--config`.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/108-api-client.md -->

# 108 — Backend API contracts and generated types (nextjs)

- **108.1** The backend contracts this app consumes are owned by the backend repositories named in
  rule 001.8. Copy them only with the project's refresh command (rule 104.5, skill
  `refresh-api-types`); never edit the copied contract or the generated types by hand. Copied
  contracts are not linted here (`CONTRACTS` stays empty for them).
- **108.2** Use the generated types for every request and response; never hand-write a type that
  duplicates a contract schema.
- **108.3** A needed API change starts in the backend repository, contract first (its own rule 108);
  the frontend task depends on it (`depends_on` in the registry, or a question until it is
  available). Do not code against fields the contract does not have.
- **108.4** After a refresh: regenerate the types, run the type check, fix every usage, update the
  mocks (MSW handlers, fixtures, stories) to the new shapes, and record the contract change in the
  work log.
- **108.5** Every status code the contract declares for an operation the UI calls has a handled UI
  state (loading, error, empty, success); unknown errors show a generic message without internal
  details (rule 005.5).
- **108.6** Route handlers and server actions of this app (`app/api/`, BFF endpoints) are an API too:
  list them in `specifications/architecture/SYSTEM_OVERVIEW.md`; if the project has its own contract
  for them, change the contract first.
- **108.7** Mock data in fixtures and stories is synthetic: no real personal data (rule 010.2).

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/110-browser-security.md -->

# 110 — Browser security and personal data (nextjs)

- **110.1** Everything that reaches the browser is public: `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variables, client
  components, files in `public/`, source maps. Secrets are server-only variables without the prefix,
  read only in server code.
- **110.2** Committed `.env*` files hold only non-secret values; secrets come from the deployment
  environment or a secret manager; `.env*.local` is never committed.
- **110.3** Personal data (rule 010, `specifications/security/PERSONAL_DATA.md`) never goes into URLs
  (paths, query parameters, redirects), analytics or tag manager events (`dataLayer`, pixels),
  console output or error trackers, and into cookies or browser storage only as documented in
  `PERSONAL_DATA.md`.
- **110.4** Adding a third-party script, iframe, pixel, font or CDN, or changing what data one
  receives, needs the developer's confirmation and an entry in `PERSONAL_DATA.md` and
  `SYSTEM_OVERVIEW.md`. Tracking that needs consent (GDPR, ePrivacy) and has no consent mechanism is
  recorded as a question, not silently added.
- **110.5** Navigation or redirects to a URL taken from a query parameter, a cookie or an API
  response are checked against an allow-list of hosts (no open redirects).
- **110.6** `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` only with content the project fully controls or sanitizes.
- **110.7** Cookies: only what the feature needs; `Secure` and `SameSite` set; `HttpOnly` when only
  the server reads them; the lifetime documented in `PERSONAL_DATA.md`.
- **110.8** Messages from iframes (`postMessage`) are accepted only after checking `event.origin`.
- **110.9** Authentication and access checks in middleware and route handlers are not weakened or
  bypassed, not even for local testing in committed code (rule 115).

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/111-testing.md -->

# 111 — Testing and checks (nextjs)

- **111.1** The gate's build steps (`BUILD_STEPS` in `agent-rules/project.sh`) run in this order:
  API type generation (if the project has it), type check, lint, tests, production build. A project
  without automated tests says so in `specifications/features-test/README.md`; adding a test
  framework is a task with its own reason (rule 004.3).
- **111.2** Test kinds for `TEST_COVERAGE.yaml`:
  - `types` — `tsc --noEmit` passes;
  - `lint` — the project's lint passes;
  - `unit` — Vitest or Jest with Testing Library, when the project has it; for the backend, Jest
    tests of a service or processor with mock services for external systems;
  - `integration` — backend tests against the Firebase emulators (111.7);
  - `story` — the component renders in Storybook with mocks for each state (loading, error, empty,
    success, partner or theme variants), when the project has Storybook;
  - `e2e` — Playwright or similar, when the project has it;
  - `build` — `next build` passes;
  - `browser` — checked in a real browser, evidence in the work log (111.3).
- **111.3** Browser check (rule 011.11, skill `verify-in-browser`): run the dev server, open every
  changed page at desktop and mobile width, go through the changed flow, check the browser console
  (no new errors or warnings) and the network calls (right endpoints and payloads; no personal data
  to third parties), and record what was seen in the work log.
- **111.4** Local runs use a local or development backend, or mocks; never a production backend with
  real personal data.
- **111.5** Run `next build` (not only `next dev`) when changing `next.config.*`, the middleware,
  environment variable usage, dependencies or server components: the dev server hides some build
  errors.
- **111.6** Start your own dev server on a free port and stop it by PID afterwards (rule 014.4).
- **111.7** Backend (rules 106, 107): services and processors are tested against the Firestore
  emulator, never with a mocked Firestore; HTTP modules with `supertest` against the functions
  emulator (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>/demo-<slug>/<region>/<module>`). The suite runs inside
  `npx firebase emulators:exec --project demo-<slug> …`, with `GCLOUD_PROJECT=demo-<slug>` set in
  the Jest setup, and `test/setup.ts` deletes every collection of `COLLECTIONS` after each test.
  External systems are the mock services (106.8); a test against a real third-party sandbox is
  named `*.real.test.ts` and is excluded from the gate.
- **111.8** With the Firebase layout the gate runs both packages: functions type check, lint,
  build and integration tests (the build comes before the tests because the functions emulator
  loads `functions/lib/`), then the UI type check, lint, tests and build (`BUILD_STEPS` in
  `agent-rules/project.sh`). Lint in the gate never uses `--fix`.
- **111.9** A new or changed endpoint has an integration test of its success path, its validation
  errors and its authorization (missing, wrong role) before it is handed to review.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/115-protected-files.md -->

# 115 — Protected setup, deployment and production files (nextjs)

Changing any of these needs the developer's explicit confirmation (rule 015).

- **115.1** CI: none exists. Nothing deploys on push; every deployment is a manual `firebase deploy`.
- **115.2** Setup and deployment: the `deploy`, `bump-version` and `prebuild` scripts of
  `crm/package.json` and `crm/scripts/`, the `deploy` scripts of `twa/package.json` and
  `functions/package.json`, `.vscode/` and `functions/.vscode/` launch settings.
- **115.3** Production routing and security: `crm/next.config.ts`, `twa/next.config.ts` (image
  remote patterns), the CORS settings and auth middleware of every controller in
  `functions/src/controllers/`.
- **115.4** Environment files and secrets: `functions/.env` (T-Bank terminal credentials, DeepSeek
  API key), `functions/.env.test-project`, `twa/.env.development`, `twa/.env.development.local`, `twa/.env.production`, and
  the environment variables of the deployed functions. Never read
  their values into logs, work logs or replies.
- **115.5** Package manager configuration (`.npmrc`, `.yarnrc*`), `engines`, `resolutions` or
  `overrides` in any `package.json`.
- **115.6** Firebase: `firebase.json` (hosting targets `crm` and `twa`, rewrites, SSR functions),
  `.firebaserc` (projects `posebestoimosti-473916` and `posebesto-business`), `firestore.indexes.json`,
  the `engines` of `functions/package.json`, the `functions/.env*` files and the secrets set with
  `firebase functions:secrets:set` (rule 107). Never deploy (`firebase deploy`) without the
  developer's explicit word.
- **115.7** Project-specific: `functions/seed-data/` (committed Firestore export used by
  `serve:test`, Q-05), the AI prompts in `functions/src/prompts/` (change product behaviour for every
  customer), the T-Bank payment and webhook code (`functions/src/services/payments/`,
  `functions/src/controllers/webhook/`), the scheduled subscription charge
  (`processSubscriptionsDaily`), and the Telegram bot token and broadcast code.

---

<!-- agent-rules/rules/201-repository-layout.md -->

# 201 — Repository layout and emulator setup of oilbot

The repository predates the boilerplate. These rules record where it differs from the stack rules
and what an agent does until each difference is resolved by a task of its own.

- **201.1** Overrides 107.1 and 107.2: the root holds the Firebase configuration and four packages
  instead of `ui/` + `functions/`: `functions/` (backend), `crm/` (staff Next.js app), `twa/`
  (customer Telegram Mini App, Next.js) and `bot/` (Telegram bot prototype). Each has its own
  `package.json` and `yarn.lock`. None of them imports from another; shared shapes come from the
  backend contract (rule 108). Everything 107 says about `ui/` applies to both `crm/` and `twa/`.
- **201.2** Overrides 107.11: hosting has two targets, `crm` (`"source": "crm"`) and `twa`
  (`"source": "twa"`), both with `frameworksBackend` in `europe-west1` and rewrites of
  `/api/public|private|admin|agent/**` to the functions of those modules before the catch-all.
- **201.3** Overrides 107.5 and 107.9: the functions tests and `serve:test` use the project id
  `test-project` (hard-coded in `functions/jest.setup.js` and `functions/src/test/setup.ts`) instead of
  `demo-oilbot`; its emulator values are in `functions/.env.test-project`. Use `test-project` for local runs
  and tests; never run the emulators against `posebestoimosti-473916` or `posebesto-business`.
  Moving to `demo-oilbot` is a task of its own.
- **201.4** Overrides 107.3: `firebase-tools` is not pinned in the root `package.json`; the gate uses
  the `firebase` CLI from `PATH`. Pinning it is a task of its own and needs the developer's word
  (rule 115.5).
- **201.5** Overrides 107.6 and 107.8: there is no root `dev` or `seed` script. Start the functions
  emulator with `cd functions && yarn serve` (or `yarn serve:test` with the committed export in
  `functions/seed-data/` and the mock AI, or `yarn serve:ai` for the same with real DeepSeek through
  `USE_REAL_AI=true`; tests always use the mock) and each app with `yarn dev` in its folder. `firebase.json` deploys only the
  Firestore indexes; there is no `firestore.rules` or `storage` block, and the Firestore emulator
  uses default rules. Adding rules is a task of its own (rule 115.6). Synthetic emulator secrets are
  in the committed `functions/.env.test-project`.
- **201.6** `twa/` is also a git repository of its own (`antonbashmakov/oilbot-ui`) whose files are
  tracked here as well (Q-01). Commit `twa/` changes in this repository only, and never run git
  commands inside `twa/` without the developer's word.
- **201.7** The API contract copies in `crm/openapi/`, `twa/openapi/` and `bot/resources/openapi/`
  have drifted from `functions/resources/openapi/` (Q-03). When a task changes an endpoint, change
  the backend contract first, then copy it to the apps that call that endpoint and regenerate their
  types (skill `refresh-api-types`).
